    "case_id": "724",
    "case_summary": "An 80-year-old woman with a history of hypertension and hyperlipidemia presented to the emergency department with progressive shortness of breath for one day duration, accompanied by chest tightness and non-radiating retrosternal chest pain. The patient denied previous episodes of chest pain, and no other relevant associated symptoms were previously noticed.\n\nInitial vital signs showed blood pressure of 191/99 mmHg, heart rate of 114 beats per minute, respiratory rate of 18 breaths per minute, and oxygen saturation of 68% under ambient air. The patient was slightly drowsy but conversant and oriented. Breathing sounds were bilaterally coarse upon auscultation. Normal heart sounds without obvious murmurs or pericardial rubs were recorded. The remainder of the systemic physical examination results were unremarkable.\n\n- Laboratory Tests: Serial Troponin-T measurements showed progressive elevation, reaching 1101 ng/L on day 4.- Imaging Studies:  1. Chest Radiography     Title: Initial chest X-ray     Description: Bilateral fine reticular opacities, widened mediastinum, cardiomegaly, and bilateral blunted costophrenic angles    2. Electrocardiogram (EKG)     Description: Sinus tachycardia with mildly elevated ST-segments at the inferior leads    3. Echocardiography     Description: Normal left atrial and ventricular sizes, good left ventricular contractility, no pericardial effusion or regional wall motion abnormalities    4. CT Angiography     Description: Right anterior mediastinal mass, well demarcated with minimal contact with surrounding great vessels and no adjoining abnormal lymph nodes    5. Coronary Angiography     Description: Diffusely slow coronary flow with patency in all three coronary arteries    6. Biopsy Results     Description: Epithelial malignancy of thymic origin, showing ovoid-to spindle neoplastic cells with nuclear atypia and mitosis",
    "frontier": [
